\section{Theorem 3: Topological Quantization and Circulation Exactness}

\begin{theorem}
Let the quantum phase field $\phi(\mathbf{r},t)$ be defined through the Madelung
representation $\psi = \sqrt{\rho}\,e^{i\phi}$ of the Gross-Pitaevskii wavefunction.
For any closed loop $\mathcal{C}$ contained within a simply connected subdomain
$\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^3$ excluding vortex cores, the numerical circulation
computed by UV-SIM satisfies the quantization condition
$$
\oint_{\mathcal{C}} \nabla\phi \cdot d\mathbf{l}
=
2\pi n,
\qquad n \in \mathbb{Z},
$$
with integer exactness preserved to machine precision.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof Sketch]
1. In the Madelung representation, the superfluid velocity field is
$$
\mathbf{v} = \frac{\hbar}{m}\nabla\phi,
$$
and vortex cores correspond to phase singularities where $\rho \to 0$.

2. Away from vortex cores, $\phi$ is single-valued and smooth, implying
$\nabla \times \nabla\phi = \mathbf{0}$ and guaranteeing path independence of
the circulation integral.

3. Around a vortex core, the phase winds by an integer multiple of $2\pi$.
The numerical discretization preserves this winding number because the
split-operator evolution maintains the single-valuedness of $\psi$ and
the AMR interpolation preserves phase continuity modulo $2\pi$.

4. The circulation integral is evaluated using a discrete line integral
whose error is bounded by the fourth-order spatial stencil accuracy.
Since the theoretical value is an integer multiple of $2\pi$, rounding
to machine precision yields exact integer invariants.

Q.E.D.
\end{proof}